From f2b67e4219f474806f15bca52b0e68b4adb8fe41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CufeHaco <redeagleteam2005@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:37:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] Keep bytes from interrupted reads and report socket EOF
 to recv_nonblock

Two IO bugs found while testing #9040. Neither depends on it; both
reproduce on stock JRuby with TCPSocket.

Thread#kill or Thread#raise can abandon IO#read(n), readpartial,
read_nonblock or sysread after a native read has already consumed bytes
from the fd. Those bytes vanished, and later reads saw the stream with a
hole in it. OpenFile#salvagePartialRead pushes them into rbuf, the buffer
every later read drains first. pread is left alone: its bytes come from
an explicit offset and do not belong in the sequential stream.

BasicSocket#recv_nonblock folded "no data yet" and EOF into one null, so
it raised EAGAIN forever once the peer had shut down (MRI returns nil).
readChannel now keeps the raw count and NONBLOCK_EOF marks EOF. On a
DatagramChannel, a zero-length datagram is data ("" in MRI), not
EAGAIN, and read_nonblock reads it as EOF as MRI does.

File.identical? with IO arguments compared paths, and a pipe or socket
has none, so File.identical?(r, r) was false for a pipe. An IO argument
is now statted through its open fd, as MRI does (reached by MRI's
socket tests once fd passing works).
